/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ Namespaces & imports: `\i <path>` compiles and runs a script like `\l`, except
/ every table/global/function it binds at its top level is qualified under
/ `.<file-stem>.*` (the file's stem) — see examples/namespace_lib.qpl and the
/ README's "Namespaces & imports" section. The qualification happens when the
/ imported script is compiled, not at run time: a bare name the file binds at
/ its own top level always refers to its own (namespaced) binding, throughout
/ the file, even in a statement that runs before that binding's statement does.
/
/ Run from the repo root:
/   cargo run -- --load-demo examples/namespaces.qpl
/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\i "namespace_lib.qpl"               / relative to this script, not to where qpl was started

log "a function imported under .namespace_lib.* — call it like any other function"
log .namespace_lib.double[21]        / bracket call — parens only needed for bareword `f x`

log "an imported global"
log .namespace_lib.greeting

log "an imported table"
.namespace_lib.lookup
